---
// GDPR (EU 2016/679) privacy page draft. Before launch:
//   1. Fill every [BRACKET] slot. The controller is whoever legally runs the site.
//      Then delete '/privacy' from UNFILLED_UNTIL_LAUNCH in
//      tests/placeholders.spec.ts (the test turns red until you do).
//   2. Make section 3 match reality: pick the self-hosted OR cloud Plausible
//      wording, or delete the analytics paragraphs entirely if the site ships
//      without analytics (then also trim the meta description below).
//   3. German-market sites: German law expects legal pages in German. German-ONLY
//      site → swap this page for the vetted German draft that ships in this
//      repo at src/pages/_datenschutz.astro (underscore = unrouted until you
//      rename it; its header says how; it serves at /datenschutz and REPLACES
//      this page — never ship both). Multilingual site via astro-i18n-setup →
//      the German privacy page lives at the locale-prefixed route (/de/privacy)
//      instead; use the German draft as its content.
//   4. Consent law: this page states no consent conclusion on purpose. Running
//      without a banner is a position, not a settled fact. The script sets no
//      cookies and writes nothing to the device, but it reads the page address and
//      the referrer in the browser and sends them to the measurement server (and
//      reads one local-storage flag, plausible_ignore, that lets you exclude your
//      own visits). The EDPB (Guidelines 2/2023 v2.0, paras 33 and 53) treats
//      JavaScript that makes the browser send information as "gaining access"
//      under Art. 5(3) ePrivacy. Germany's § 25 TDDDG (formerly TTDSG) has no
//      analytics exception, and the DSK's OH Digitale Dienste v1.2 (Nov 2024,
//      Rz. 87 to 90) says reach measurement is not consent-free as such.
//      Plausible's own legal assessment argues from "stores nothing" and does not
//      address access. Other EU markets have their own ePrivacy transpositions.
//      Have the position reviewed for your market. ANY third-party embed (YouTube
//      iframe, remote Google Fonts), another analytics tool, or wider measurement
//      (optional measurements such as outbound links, file downloads or form
//      submissions; custom properties) changes the answer AND the text: re-check
//      this page BEFORE shipping such an addition.
//      Base.astro also reads a 'theme' key from local storage, and nothing in the
//      template writes it. If you add a toggle that stores the choice, say so in
//      section 3.
//   5. A solid baseline, not legal advice. When in doubt, have it reviewed.
// The copy below already passes the a11y / seo / tone / email suites: keep one
// <h1>, the description in the 120-160 char window, no em dashes or banned
// contractions, and every email address behind <EmailLink>.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import EmailLink from '../components/EmailLink.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config';
---
<Base
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description={`How ${SITE.name} handles personal data: cookieless analytics, hosting on Cloudflare Pages, contact data handling, and your rights under the GDPR.`}>
  <section class="wrap">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p>
      Last updated: [DATE]. This page explains which personal data this website
      processes, on what legal basis under the GDPR (EU 2016/679), and which
      rights you have.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Controller</h2>
    <p>Responsible for data processing on this website:</p>
    <p>
      [CONTROLLER LEGAL NAME]<br />
      [STREET AND NUMBER]<br />
      [POSTCODE, CITY, COUNTRY]<br />
      Contact: <EmailLink address="hello@example.com" subject="Privacy request" />
    </p>

    <h2>2. Hosting and server logs</h2>
    <p>
      This site is served by <strong>Cloudflare Pages</strong> (Cloudflare, Inc.,
      101 Townsend St, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA). When you visit a page,
      Cloudflare processes technical connection data (IP address, date and time,
      requested URL, browser user agent) to deliver the site and to protect it
      against attacks. The legal basis is our legitimate interest in operating a
      secure and fast website (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). Cloudflare acts as our
      processor under Art. 28 GDPR; transfers to the USA are covered by the
      EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and standard contractual clauses. We do not
      combine these logs with any other data.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Cookies and analytics</h2>
    <p>
      This website is ad-free and <strong>sets no cookies</strong>. Your visit
      leaves no advertising or cross-site tracking identifiers with us.
    </p>
    <p>
      To understand how the site is used, we run <strong>Plausible
      Analytics</strong>, a privacy-friendly measurement tool
      [CHOOSE ONE: self-hosted on our own server / operated for us as our processor
      (Art. 28 GDPR) by Plausible Insights O&Uuml;, V&auml;striku tn 2, 50403 Tartu,
      Estonia (EU)]. Its script sets no cookies and writes nothing to your
      device. When you open a page, it sends the address of that page and the
      page you came from to the measurement server, together with the technical
      data your browser sends with every request (browser, operating system,
      device type, IP address). When you leave the page, it also sends how far
      you scrolled and how long the page was in the foreground. The tool derives
      your approximate location (country, region and city) from the IP address
      and does not store the IP address itself. From these data it forms a
      short-lived identifier that is replaced every 24 hours, so a visit is
      counted once a day and cannot be followed across days or across websites.
      The results are aggregated counts, for example page views, referrers and
      countries. No data is passed on to third parties for their own purposes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding how the
      website is used and in improving it (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). You can prevent
      the measurement by blocking the analytics script in your browser.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Contact by email</h2>
    <p>
      If you contact us, we process the data you provide (name, email address,
      and the content of your message) to handle the request and possible
      follow-up questions. The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR where the request
      relates to a contract, otherwise our legitimate interest in answering
      enquiries (Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR). We keep the correspondence only as long as
      needed for this purpose and for any statutory retention duties.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you have the right of access (Art. 15), rectification
      (Art. 16), erasure (Art. 17), restriction of processing (Art. 18), data
      portability (Art. 20), and objection to processing based on legitimate
      interest (Art. 21). To exercise any of these rights, contact us via the
      address in section 1; we will verify your identity before acting on a
      request. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR).
    </p>

    <h2>6. Changes to this page</h2>
    <p>
      We will update this page whenever the data processing on this site
      changes, and adjust the date at the top accordingly.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
